"Stacks time-varying components around a shifting baseline to emphasize changing band shapes rather than a fixed total axis.\n\nUse when: Broad temporal composition patterns matter more than exact totals. Nonnegative components form a continuous, visually readable set of bands.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need precise values, a zero baseline, or direct comparisons of component levels.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time. Band thickness encodes component magnitude; baseline displacement is a layout calculation rather than data.\n\nBuild: Align all component values on one time grid. Choose and document a streamgraph baseline and stable layer order. Compute band boundaries, apply distinguishable fills, and label only wide clear sections.\n\nCheck: Check each band’s thickness equals its value at every time. Verify negative components are excluded or handled by a justified alternative. State that vertical center position has no direct quantitative interpretation.\n\nPitfalls: A floating baseline hides the total and makes slope comparisons difficult. Prefer a conventional stacked area or separate line charts when quantitative reading is important.\n\nExample: Show synthetic listening volume by music category across a year, emphasizing seasonal rises and falls with a disclosed centered baseline.\n\nAlternatives: Stacked area chart, Lasagna plot, Ridgeline plot."
